<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Bates Thurston</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir John Bates Thurston was a British colonial official who served as a minister in Cakobau's Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> before the cession of 1874. He then rose in the British colonial service to become governor of Fiji and High Commissioner for the Western Pacific from 1888 until his death in 1897.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-bates-thurston.jpg" alt="John Bates Thurston" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Bates_Thurston_(1836%E2%80%931897).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Bates Thurston was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 31 January 1836 and went to sea in 1850 after an elementary education. In 1855 he became a first officer, but he soon fell ill with cholera and was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> to recover, where he farmed sheep with a friend until a flood destroyed the farm in 1862. In 1864 he joined a botanical expedition to the South Sea Islands, was wrecked off Samoa and stranded for eighteen months before being brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He found work at the British Consulate and in 1869 became acting consul for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>. In June 1871 he brokered an arrangement between the Bauan chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seru-epenisa-cakobau">Seru Epenisa Cakobau</a> and the British settlers, persuading the chiefs to accept a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with Cakobau as king but real power in a settler-dominated cabinet and legislature. Overspending soon left an unmanageable debt, and in 1872, at Cakobau's request, Thurston offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the cession of the islands. As Premier from 23 March to 10 October 1874 he saw the negotiations through to the formal cession.</p><p>As Colonial Secretary he drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-sugar-refining-company">Colonial Sugar Refining Company</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> with 2,000 acres of land for its plantations, and in 1879 he became secretary to the High Commissioner. He was made Governor of Fiji in February 1888. He founded the Suva Botanical Gardens, later named Thurston Gardens, and the palm Pritchardia thurstonii bears his name; he was a fellow of the Linnean and Geographical societies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His health failed in 1895 and he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on leave. Returning to his post in 1896, he died at sea aboard the S.S. Burrumbeet between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> on 7 February 1897, and was buried in Melbourne General Cemetery, where the Fijian government pays for the upkeep of his grave. The British government granted his widow Amelia a civil list pension, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> paid a pension to each of his five children while they were minors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Bates Thurston” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom had a cabinet dominated by Europeans, among them <b>John Bates Thurston</b>, and a legislature in which settlers held great influence.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fiji">Kingdom of Fiji</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suva">Suva</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/fiji">Fiji</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1875" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Bates Thurston"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1875">What was here in 1875? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
